The prior isp(x) = \mathcal{N}(x; x_b, B), \text{ with } x \in \mathbb{R}^n

where x_b is the background mean and B the background-error covariance. The likelihood is the observation model 

(2.1) with a linear H,p(y \mid x) = \mathcal{N}(y; H x, R), \text{ with } H \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}

where R is the observation-error covariance. By the standard Gaussian conjugacy result, the posterior isp(x \mid y) = \mathcal{N}(x; x^*, P^*)

with meanx^* = x_b + K(y - H x_b), \text{ with } K = B H^\top (H B H^\top + R)^{-1}

where K is the Kalman gain and y - H x_b the innovation, and covarianceP^* = (I - K H) B = (B^{-1} + H^\top R^{-1} H)^{-1}

The two forms of P^* are equivalent by Sherman-Morrison-Woodbury, and which one to compute depends on which is cheaper.

